/* patho-qbank.js - six questions per BIO 280 module.

   Written for this course rather than borrowed. Nothing here is copied from a
   publisher's bank, and no question carries a chapter citation, because the
   chapter would have to be invented to write one.

   Read by nur258-module.js, which takes window.ABSN_QBANK first and falls back
   to NUR258_QBANK. Same shape as that bank: q, type, opts, ans, why.

   Every 'why' explains the distractor as well as the answer - the wrong option
   a student would reach for is usually the more useful thing to learn. */
window.ABSN_QBANK={"1": [{"q": "A client is admitted with a serum sodium of 118 mEq/L, a serum osmolality of 250 mOsm/kg, and concentrated urine with a high specific gravity. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diabetes insipidus", "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH)", "Dehydration from vomiting", "Diabetic ketoacidosis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Too much ADH means water is reabsorbed and retained. That <b>dilutes</b> the sodium and the serum osmolality while making the urine small and concentrated - low serum, high urine. Diabetes insipidus is the exact opposite: no ADH, so huge dilute urine and a <b>rising</b> serum sodium and osmolality. The clue that separates them is which fluid is concentrated."}, {"q": "A client with a potassium of 6.8 mEq/L is prescribed regular insulin with dextrose. What is the rationale?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Insulin binds potassium in the bloodstream", "Insulin drives potassium from the blood into the cells", "Insulin increases renal excretion of potassium", "Dextrose chelates the excess potassium"], "ans": [1], "why": "Insulin shifts potassium <b>into</b> the cells, which drops the serum level within minutes. The dextrose is there only to stop the insulin causing hypoglycaemia. Note what this does <i>not</i> do: the potassium is still in the body, just relocated - so it buys time while a definitive removal method works."}, {"q": "A client has an arterial blood gas of pH 7.28, PaCO2 58 mmHg, HCO3 25 mEq/L. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Metabolic acidosis, uncompensated", "Respiratory acidosis, uncompensated", "Respiratory alkalosis, partially compensated", "Metabolic alkalosis, uncompensated"], "ans": [1], "why": "pH is low so it is an acidosis. The CO2 is <b>high</b> and moves opposite the pH, which makes it <b>respiratory</b> - ROME, Respiratory Opposite. The bicarbonate is still normal, so the kidneys have not started compensating yet: uncompensated. Kidneys take hours to days, which is why an acute respiratory problem shows a normal bicarbonate."}, {"q": "Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with hypokalemia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Muscle weakness and cramping", "Peaked T waves on the ECG", "Flattened T waves and a U wave", "Paralytic ileus and constipation", "Diarrhoea and intestinal cramping"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Low potassium makes muscle - including smooth muscle - weak and sluggish, so the gut slows to an <b>ileus</b>. The ECG flattens the T wave and adds a <b>U wave</b>. <b>Peaked</b> T waves and hyperactive bowel belong to hyper<i>kalemia</i>, which speeds the gut up instead. Both ends of the range cause weakness, so weakness alone will not tell you which."}, {"q": "A client with liver failure has generalised oedema and ascites. Which mechanism is primarily responsible?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure", "Decreased colloidal osmotic (oncotic) pressure", "Increased vascular permeability", "Lymphatic obstruction"], "ans": [1], "why": "The failing liver cannot make <b>albumin</b>, and albumin is what generates the oncotic <i>pulling</i> force that holds fluid inside the vessel. Without it, fluid leaks into the interstitium. Hydrostatic pressure is a <i>pushing</i> force and is the mechanism in heart failure; increased permeability is the mechanism in sepsis and burns."}, {"q": "A client has been vomiting for two days. Which acid-base disturbance does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Vomiting loses gastric <b>acid</b>, so what remains is relatively alkaline: metabolic alkalosis. Diarrhoea is the mirror image - it loses intestinal <b>bicarbonate</b> and causes metabolic acidosis. Both lose potassium, so the electrolyte is the same and the acid-base result is opposite."}], "2": [{"q": "A client is neutropenic after chemotherapy and has a single temperature of 38.2 C. The client appears comfortable and has no other complaints. What is the priority interpretation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Expected after chemotherapy; continue to monitor", "A medical emergency requiring immediate evaluation", "A transfusion reaction", "A normal inflammatory response"], "ans": [1], "why": "With too few neutrophils there are not enough cells to <b>make</b> pus, redness or swelling, so the usual signs of infection are absent. Fever may be the only sign the body can still produce, and it is treated as sepsis until proven otherwise. Looking well is not reassurance here - it is the expected appearance."}, {"q": "A client's differential shows a marked increase in eosinophils. Which conditions are consistent with this? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Allergic reaction", "Parasitic infection", "Acute bacterial pneumonia", "Asthma", "Viral hepatitis"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Eosinophils rise in <b>allergy and parasites</b> - asthma is an allergic process, so it belongs. Acute bacterial infection raises <b>neutrophils</b>; viral infection raises <b>lymphocytes</b>. Reading the differential by which cell is raised is faster and more reliable than reading the total white count."}, {"q": "Which statement correctly describes adaptive immunity?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It responds within minutes and works the same way every time", "It is present at birth and includes the skin and stomach acid", "It is specific, takes days on first exposure, and creates memory", "It relies mainly on neutrophils and natural killer cells"], "ans": [2], "why": "Adaptive immunity is learned, <b>specific</b>, and it <b>remembers</b> - which is why the second exposure is milder and why vaccines work. The other options describe <b>innate</b> immunity: immediate, non-specific, no memory, and staffed by neutrophils, macrophages and NK cells."}, {"q": "An 82-year-old is brought in with new confusion and two falls this week. She is afebrile and denies burning on urination. Which possibility should the nurse consider first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Early dementia", "Urinary tract infection", "Normal ageing", "Depression"], "ans": [1], "why": "New confusion or a sudden functional decline in an older adult is a <b>UTI until proven otherwise</b>, and it often comes with no dysuria, no urgency and no fever - the blunted immune response and altered temperature regulation take the classic signs away. Dementia comes on over months and does not appear in a week."}, {"q": "A urinalysis is positive for leukocyte esterase and nitrites. What does this indicate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["White blood cells and gram-negative bacteria are present", "The specimen was contaminated", "Glomerular damage", "Dehydration"], "ans": [0], "why": "Leukocyte esterase indicates <b>white cells</b>; nitrites indicate bacteria that convert nitrate to nitrite, which <b>gram-negative</b> organisms such as <i>E. coli</i> do. Together they support infection. A culture still follows, because it names the organism and its sensitivities - and it should be collected <i>before</i> antibiotics start."}, {"q": "Which client would the nurse expect to be prescribed an antibiotic?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with influenza A", "A client with a rhinovirus cold", "A client with bacterial pyelonephritis", "A client with oral candidiasis"], "ans": [2], "why": "Antibiotics act on <b>bacteria</b>. Influenza and rhinovirus are viruses; candidiasis is a fungus and needs an antifungal. Giving an antibiotic for a viral illness does not help the client and it breeds resistance - the most commonly tested point in this module."}], "3": [{"q": "A long-term smoker's bronchial biopsy shows the normal ciliated columnar epithelium replaced by squamous epithelium. What is this called?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hyperplasia", "Metaplasia", "Dysplasia", "Anaplasia"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Metaplasia</b> is one mature cell type being replaced by another that tolerates the stress better - here, tougher squamous cells replacing fragile ciliated ones. It is reversible if the smoking stops. <b>Dysplasia</b> would be disordered, abnormal growth and is the pre-cancerous step; <b>anaplasia</b> is the complete loss of differentiation seen in malignancy."}, {"q": "Which characteristic definitively distinguishes a malignant tumour from a benign one?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rate of growth", "Ability to metastasise", "Size of the tumour", "Presence of pain"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Metastasis</b> is the defining feature - benign tumours never metastasise. Benign tumours can be large, can grow quickly and can hurt if they press on something, so none of those separate the two reliably. Invasion and lack of a capsule are the other malignant hallmarks."}, {"q": "Which statements about apoptosis are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["It is programmed and normal", "The cell swells and ruptures", "It does not cause inflammation", "Cell contents are contained and cleared by macrophages", "It always affects large groups of cells"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Apoptosis is controlled: the cell <b>shrinks</b>, is packaged up, and is cleared without spilling its contents - so there is <b>no inflammation</b>, and it usually affects single cells. Swelling, rupture, spilled contents and inflammation describe <b>necrosis</b>, which is unplanned injury."}, {"q": "What is the most common cause of cellular injury?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypoxia", "Radiation", "Genetic mutation", "Nutritional excess"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Hypoxia</b> is the commonest cause. Without oxygen there is no ATP; without ATP the sodium-potassium pump fails, sodium and water flood in, the cell swells, and lysosomes rupture. Every other cause on this list is real but far less frequent."}, {"q": "A client asks why cancer that started in the colon was found in the liver. What is the best explanation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The client developed a second, unrelated cancer", "Malignant cells travelled through the bloodstream and lymphatics", "The liver is where all cancers begin", "The biopsy was likely in error"], "ans": [1], "why": "Metastasis travels by the <b>bloodstream</b>, the <b>lymphatics</b>, and by direct seeding of a body cavity. The liver, lung, bone and brain are the commonest destinations because they receive the most blood flow. It is the same cancer in a new place, not a new one."}, {"q": "A client's muscle mass decreases markedly after six weeks in a cast. What cellular adaptation has occurred?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Atrophy", "Hypertrophy", "Hyperplasia", "Dysplasia"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Atrophy</b> is cells shrinking in response to less work, less blood supply or less stimulation - and it is reversible once the limb is used again. Hypertrophy is cells getting bigger, hyperplasia is more cells, and dysplasia is disordered growth."}], "4": [{"q": "A client with a suspected infection has a temperature of 38.9 C, heart rate 112, respiratory rate 24, and a white count of 15,000. Blood pressure is 88/50 after 2 litres of fluid, and lactate is rising. How is this best classified?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Systemic inflammatory response syndrome only", "Sepsis", "Septic shock", "Uncomplicated infection"], "ans": [2], "why": "Hypotension that <b>persists after adequate fluid</b>, with a rising lactate, is <b>septic shock</b>. SIRS is the vital-sign criteria alone and needs no infection; sepsis is infection plus organ dysfunction. The fluid trial is what separates sepsis from septic shock, so the timing of the blood pressure matters."}, {"q": "Which finding is characteristic of EARLY septic shock?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cold, clammy, pale skin", "Warm, flushed skin with a bounding pulse", "Narrow pulse pressure", "Bradycardia"], "ans": [1], "why": "Distributive shock dilates the vessels, so blood sits at the surface: the skin is <b>warm and flushed</b>, the pulse bounding, the pulse pressure wide. This is the trap - the client does not look like shock. Cold and clammy is what every <i>other</i> shock looks like, and what septic shock becomes later once compensation fails."}, {"q": "Which findings distinguish rheumatoid arthritis from osteoarthritis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Symmetric joint involvement", "Morning stiffness lasting more than an hour", "Stiffness that worsens with use through the day", "Systemic features such as fever and fatigue", "Heberden nodes on the distal finger joints"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "RA is <b>autoimmune and systemic</b>: symmetric small joints, morning stiffness over an hour that <i>improves</i> with use, plus fever, fatigue and weight loss. Stiffness that worsens with use, and Heberden nodes, are <b>osteoarthritis</b> - a wear problem with no systemic features."}, {"q": "A client describes burning, shooting, electric pain in both feet. Which type of pain is this, and what is the implication for treatment?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Somatic pain; responds well to opioids", "Visceral pain; responds well to antispasmodics", "Neuropathic pain; responds poorly to opioids", "Referred pain; requires no treatment"], "ans": [2], "why": "Burning, shooting and electric are the signature of <b>neuropathic</b> pain - the nerve itself is damaged. It responds <b>poorly to opioids</b> and is usually treated with anticonvulsants or antidepressants. Somatic pain is sharp and well localised; visceral pain is deep, cramping and poorly localised."}, {"q": "A surgical wound has separated and a loop of bowel is visible. What is the nurse's immediate action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Attempt to replace the bowel and apply a dry dressing", "Cover with sterile saline-soaked gauze and call the surgeon", "Apply firm pressure to close the wound", "Position the client flat with legs extended"], "ans": [1], "why": "Evisceration is covered with <b>sterile saline-soaked</b> gauze to keep the tissue moist, and the surgeon is called. Nothing is ever pushed back in, and a dry dressing would adhere and damage the bowel. Position low Fowler with knees bent to reduce tension on the wound."}, {"q": "Which client has the classic presentation of Parkinson disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Weakness that worsens with use and improves with rest", "Resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia and postural instability", "Symmetric ascending weakness after a viral illness", "Relapsing optic neuritis and numbness in a young adult"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>TRAP</b> - Tremor at rest, Rigidity, Akinesia/bradykinesia, Postural instability - from loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra. Weakness worse with use is <b>myasthenia gravis</b>; relapsing optic neuritis in a young adult is <b>multiple sclerosis</b>."}], "5": [{"q": "An 80-year-old develops confusion over 12 hours that fluctuates and is worse at night. Attention is markedly impaired. Which is the most likely explanation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Alzheimer dementia", "Delirium", "Normal ageing", "Major depression"], "ans": [1], "why": "Onset over <b>hours</b>, a <b>fluctuating</b> course worse at night, and <b>impaired attention</b> are the defining features of delirium - and it is <b>reversible</b> if the cause is found. Dementia develops over months to years with a steady course and attention preserved until late. Delirium is a medical emergency, not a psychiatric one."}, {"q": "Which are POSITIVE symptoms of schizophrenia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Auditory hallucinations", "Flat affect", "Delusions", "Avolition", "Disorganised speech"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Positive symptoms are things <b>added</b> to normal experience - hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech. Negative symptoms are things <b>taken away</b> - flat affect, avolition, alogia, anhedonia. The distinction matters clinically: negative symptoms respond far less well to medication and predict the worse long-term outcome."}, {"q": "A client with a suspected stroke arrives 90 minutes after symptom onset. What must occur before thrombolytic therapy?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A lumbar puncture", "A non-contrast CT of the head", "An MRI with contrast", "A carotid ultrasound"], "ans": [1], "why": "A <b>non-contrast CT</b> must rule out haemorrhage first. Ischaemic and haemorrhagic strokes look identical at the bedside, and a thrombolytic given in a haemorrhagic stroke is catastrophic. This is why the scan comes before the drug, every time, however clear the picture seems."}, {"q": "Which finding is the EARLIEST indicator of increasing intracranial pressure?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A change in level of consciousness", "A fixed dilated pupil", "Cushing triad", "Projectile vomiting"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>change in level of consciousness</b> is the earliest and most sensitive sign. Cushing triad - rising systolic with a widening pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular respirations - is a <b>late and ominous</b> finding, as is a fixed dilated pupil. Waiting for those means waiting too long."}, {"q": "During a generalised tonic-clonic seizure, which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Protect the head and turn the client to the side", "Insert a padded tongue blade in the mouth", "Time the seizure", "Restrain the limbs to prevent injury", "Loosen restrictive clothing"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Protect, position on the side for airway drainage, time it, and loosen clothing. <b>Never restrain</b> the client and <b>never put anything in the mouth</b> - both cause injury and neither prevents one. A seizure continuing beyond 5 minutes is status epilepticus and an emergency."}, {"q": "A client is prescribed a medication that increases serotonin. Which neurotransmitter imbalance is most associated with the positive symptoms of psychosis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Too little serotonin", "Too much dopamine", "Too little GABA", "Too much acetylcholine"], "ans": [1], "why": "Excess <b>dopamine</b> activity underlies the positive symptoms - hallucinations and delusions - which is why antipsychotics block dopamine. Too little dopamine gives Parkinson and the negative symptoms. Too little GABA lowers the seizure threshold, since GABA is the brain's brake."}], "6": [{"q": "A client has a full-thickness burn over the anterior chest and abdomen. The area is white, leathery and dry, and the client reports no pain there. How should the nurse interpret the absence of pain?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The burn is superficial and healing well", "Analgesia is adequate", "Nerve endings have been destroyed, indicating a deeper burn", "The client is in denial"], "ans": [2], "why": "Painless is <b>worse</b>, not better. Full-thickness burns destroy the nerve endings, so the deepest areas hurt least. The <b>most painful</b> burn is partial thickness, where nerve endings are exposed but intact. Never read absent pain as a good sign."}, {"q": "An adult weighing 80 kg has burns to the entire front torso, the entire right arm and the entire right leg. Using the rule of nines, what is the total body surface area burned?", "type": "single", "opts": ["27%", "36%", "45%", "54%"], "ans": [2], "why": "Front torso <b>18%</b> + one whole arm <b>9%</b> + one whole leg <b>18%</b> = <b>45%</b>. The commonest error is halving the leg - a whole leg is 18% (front 9 + back 9), and only the front alone would be 9%. Reading which surfaces are involved is half the question."}, {"q": "Using the Parkland formula for the 80 kg client with 45% TBSA burns, how much fluid should be given in the FIRST 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["3,600 mL", "7,200 mL", "14,400 mL", "1,800 mL"], "ans": [1], "why": "4 x 80 x 45 = <b>14,400 mL</b> total for 24 hours. <b>Half</b> goes in the first 8 hours = <b>7,200 mL</b>, and the other half over the following 16. The clock starts at the <b>time of the burn</b>, not at arrival - if two hours have passed, that first half must still be complete by the 8-hour mark."}, {"q": "Which finding best indicates that fluid resuscitation is working in a burn client?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood pressure returning to baseline", "Heart rate below 100", "Urine output of 30 to 50 mL per hour", "Clearing of the sensorium"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Urine output</b> is the perfusion measure the exam wants - 30 to 50 mL/hr in adults, 1 mL/kg/hr in children. Blood pressure and heart rate are maintained by compensation long after perfusion has started to fail, so they reassure too early. Parkland gives a <i>starting</i> rate that is then titrated to output."}, {"q": "A client reports severe pain in the lower leg six hours after a tibial fracture. The pain is unrelieved by opioids and worsens sharply on passive stretch of the toes. Pulses are present. What is the priority interpretation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Expected post-fracture pain", "Compartment syndrome", "Fat embolism", "Deep vein thrombosis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pain <b>out of proportion</b>, unrelieved by opioids and worse on passive stretch is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise. <b>Present pulses do not rule it out</b> - pulselessness is the <i>last</i> of the six Ps, and waiting for it means waiting until the muscle is dead. Do not elevate above the heart and do not ice."}, {"q": "Which pressure injury descriptions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Stage 1: intact skin with non-blanchable redness", "Stage 2: full thickness with visible subcutaneous fat", "Stage 3: full thickness with subcutaneous fat visible, bone not exposed", "Stage 4: bone, tendon or muscle exposed", "Unstageable: base obscured by slough or eschar"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Stage 2 is <b>partial</b> thickness - a shallow ulcer or blister with the dermis visible and no slough. Visible subcutaneous fat makes it stage 3. An injury cannot be staged while slough or eschar hides the base, which is exactly what unstageable means."}], "7": [{"q": "Two unaffected parents have a child with cystic fibrosis. What is the chance that their next child will be affected?", "type": "single", "opts": ["0%", "25%", "50%", "100%"], "ans": [1], "why": "Cystic fibrosis is <b>autosomal recessive</b>. Both parents are unaffected carriers, so each pregnancy carries a <b>25%</b> chance of an affected child, 50% carrier, 25% clear. Recessive conditions skip generations, which is why neither parent has it. The 50% figure belongs to autosomal <i>dominant</i> inheritance."}, {"q": "A pedigree shows a condition appearing in every generation, affecting males and females equally, with an affected parent for every affected child. Which inheritance pattern is this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Autosomal recessive", "Autosomal dominant", "X-linked recessive", "Mitochondrial"], "ans": [1], "why": "Appearing in <b>every generation</b> with equal sex distribution and an affected parent each time is <b>autosomal dominant</b> - one copy is enough. Recessive conditions skip generations. X-linked recessive would affect mainly males and would show no male-to-male transmission."}, {"q": "Which statements about X-linked recessive inheritance are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Males are affected more often than females", "An affected father passes the condition to all of his sons", "Carrier mothers pass the allele to half of their sons", "Haemophilia A follows this pattern", "There is no male-to-male transmission"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "A father gives his son a <b>Y</b>, never his X - so male-to-male transmission is impossible, and an affected father cannot pass it to his sons at all. That single fact answers most pedigree questions. Carrier mothers pass the allele to half their sons and half their daughters."}, {"q": "A client is 5 weeks pregnant. Why is this the period of greatest concern for teratogen exposure?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The fetus is growing fastest at this point", "Weeks 3 to 8 are organogenesis, when structural malformation occurs", "The placenta is fully formed and concentrates toxins", "Maternal blood volume peaks at this time"], "ans": [1], "why": "Weeks <b>3 to 8</b> are organogenesis and the period of greatest vulnerability to structural malformation - and many women do not yet know they are pregnant. Before implantation it is all-or-nothing; after week 8 the risk shifts to growth restriction and functional problems, particularly of the CNS."}, {"q": "A client with a 45, XO karyotype would be expected to have which features?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Tall stature, small testes, gynaecomastia", "Short stature, webbed neck, absent spontaneous puberty", "Intellectual disability and a single palmar crease", "Chorea and psychiatric decline in the fourth decade"], "ans": [1], "why": "45, XO is <b>Turner syndrome</b> - a female with one X. Short stature, webbed neck, no spontaneous puberty, infertility and coarctation. 47, XXY is <b>Klinefelter</b>: tall, small testes, gynaecomastia. Trisomy 21 gives the single palmar crease; Huntington gives the chorea."}, {"q": "When should folic acid supplementation begin to prevent neural tube defects?", "type": "single", "opts": ["As soon as pregnancy is confirmed", "Before conception", "At the start of the second trimester", "In the third trimester"], "ans": [1], "why": "The neural tube closes by about <b>week 4</b>, which is often before a woman knows she is pregnant - so starting after a positive test is already late. Supplementation of 400 to 800 mcg daily should begin <b>before conception</b> in anyone who could become pregnant."}], "8": [{"q": "A client has a TSH of 12 mIU/L and a free T4 below the reference range. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Primary hypothyroidism", "Secondary hypothyroidism", "Primary hyperthyroidism", "Graves disease"], "ans": [0], "why": "In <b>primary</b> disease the problem is the gland itself: the thyroid cannot answer, so the pituitary shouts louder and <b>TSH rises</b> while T4 falls. If the pituitary were the problem, <b>both</b> would be low. One high and one low points at the target gland; both low points upstream."}, {"q": "Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with hyperthyroidism? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Weight loss despite a good appetite", "Cold intolerance and constipation", "Heat intolerance and diarrhoea", "Tachycardia and tremor", "Bradycardia and dry skin"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Hyperthyroidism runs everything <b>fast and hot</b>: weight loss with a good appetite, heat intolerance, diarrhoea, tachycardia, tremor, anxiety and insomnia. Cold intolerance, constipation, bradycardia and dry skin are hypo<i>thyroid</i> - slow and cold. One axis, two directions."}, {"q": "Two days after a thyroidectomy, a client reports tingling around the mouth and in the fingertips, and has a positive Chvostek sign. What is the nurse's priority concern?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury", "Hypercalcaemia from bone release", "Hyperkalaemia", "Thyroid storm"], "ans": [0], "why": "The parathyroids sit on the thyroid and are easily injured or removed during surgery. Losing PTH drops the calcium, and low calcium makes everything <b>irritable</b>: perioral tingling, cramps, Chvostek and Trousseau signs - and the one that kills, <b>laryngospasm</b>. Keep calcium gluconate available."}, {"q": "A client with Addison disease presents in crisis. Which set of laboratory findings is expected?", "type": "single", "opts": ["High sodium, low potassium, high glucose", "Low sodium, high potassium, low glucose", "High sodium, high potassium, high glucose", "Low sodium, low potassium, low glucose"], "ans": [1], "why": "Too little cortisol and aldosterone means sodium and water are <b>lost</b> and potassium is <b>retained</b>, with hypoglycaemia from absent cortisol: <b>low sodium, high potassium, low glucose</b>, plus profound hypotension. Cushing is the mirror image - sodium retained, potassium low, glucose high."}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes has a glucose of 480 mg/dL, pH 7.19, and deep rapid respirations with fruity breath. Which is the priority initial intervention?", "type": "single", "opts": ["IV regular insulin bolus", "IV normal saline", "IV sodium bicarbonate", "Subcutaneous long-acting insulin"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Fluid first.</b> Osmotic diuresis has emptied the intravascular space for hours, and fluid alone begins lowering the glucose while protecting perfusion to the kidneys and brain. Insulin comes second - and only once the <b>potassium</b> is known, because insulin drives potassium into the cells and can drop it far enough to stop the heart."}, {"q": "A client in DKA has a glucose falling to 250 mg/dL on an insulin infusion. What should happen next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stop the insulin infusion", "Add dextrose to the IV fluid and continue the insulin", "Give a bolus of long-acting insulin", "Stop all IV fluids"], "ans": [1], "why": "The insulin is not there only to lower the number - it is there to <b>switch off ketone production</b>, and that job is not finished when the glucose looks better. Adding <b>dextrose</b> lets the insulin keep running without causing hypoglycaemia. Stopping it early restarts ketogenesis."}], "9": [{"q": "A client has a haemoglobin of 9.1 g/dL with an elevated MCV, and reports numbness and tingling in both feet with an unsteady gait. Which deficiency is most likely?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Iron", "Folate", "Vitamin B12", "Vitamin K"], "ans": [2], "why": "Macrocytic anaemia narrows it to <b>B12 or folate</b>, and the <b>neurological</b> signs - numbness, tingling, gait and balance problems - belong to <b>B12</b>. Folate deficiency gives the same blood picture with no neurological findings. Treating with folate alone corrects the count and lets the nerve damage continue silently."}, {"q": "Which laboratory results support a diagnosis of disseminated intravascular coagulation? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Low platelet count", "Elevated D-dimer", "Low fibrinogen", "Shortened PT and aPTT", "Prolonged PT and aPTT"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "DIC clots and bleeds at the same time: widespread microthrombi <b>consume</b> platelets and clotting factors, so platelets and fibrinogen fall while PT and aPTT are <b>prolonged</b>, and D-dimer rises from all the clot breakdown. Shortened clotting times would mean the opposite of consumption."}, {"q": "A client in sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis is in severe pain. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["IV fluids", "Supplemental oxygen", "Cold packs to the painful areas", "Opioid analgesia", "Keeping the client warm"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Hydration, oxygen, warmth and adequate analgesia - opioids are entirely appropriate here. <b>Cold triggers sickling</b> by causing vasoconstriction, so cold packs make the crisis worse. Dehydration does the same, which is why fluids come first."}, {"q": "Which bleeding pattern suggests a platelet problem rather than a clotting factor deficiency?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Bleeding into joints and deep muscle", "Petechiae, purpura and mucosal bleeding", "Delayed bleeding after surgery", "Retroperitoneal haemorrhage"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Platelet problems bleed into the skin and mucosa</b> - petechiae, purpura, gums, nosebleeds. <b>Factor problems bleed deep</b> - into joints and muscle, and often with a delay after injury or surgery, which is the haemophilia pattern. That distinction sorts most questions before you read the labs."}, {"q": "A client's blood pressure is consistently 146/92. How is this classified?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevated", "Stage 1 hypertension", "Stage 2 hypertension", "Hypertensive crisis"], "ans": [2], "why": "Stage 2 is systolic <b>140 or above</b> OR diastolic <b>90 or above</b> - and this reading meets both. Stage 1 is 130-139 or 80-89. Elevated is 120-129 with a diastolic under 80. Crisis is above 180 and/or above 120. Note the <b>or</b>: the higher of the two numbers decides the stage."}, {"q": "Why is hypertension described as a silent disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It causes no arterial damage until late", "It damages arteries and organs for years without producing symptoms", "It can only be detected by echocardiogram", "Symptoms appear only during a crisis and then resolve"], "ans": [1], "why": "The damage is continuous and <b>asymptomatic</b> - the heart, brain, kidneys, eyes and vessels are being injured for years while the client feels well. Headache and nosebleed are late findings, not early warnings, which is why screening rather than symptoms is what finds it."}], "10": [{"q": "A client reports crushing chest pain at rest for 40 minutes, unrelieved by three nitroglycerin tablets. Troponin is elevated and the ECG shows ST elevation. What has occurred?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stable angina", "Unstable angina", "ST-elevation myocardial infarction", "Pericarditis"], "ans": [2], "why": "An elevated <b>troponin</b> means myocardial necrosis - that is what makes it an infarct rather than angina. <b>ST elevation</b> means full-thickness occlusion, so the priority is opening the vessel: PCI ideally within 90 minutes. Unstable angina has the same rest pain but a <b>normal</b> troponin."}, {"q": "A client with an inferior wall MI becomes profoundly hypotensive after nitroglycerin. What is the most likely explanation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An allergic reaction to nitroglycerin", "Right ventricular involvement, which is preload dependent", "The dose was too low", "Concurrent beta blocker therapy"], "ans": [1], "why": "Inferior MIs are usually right coronary artery, and often involve the <b>right ventricle</b>, which depends heavily on <b>preload</b>. Nitrates drop preload, and in an RV infarct that causes catastrophic hypotension. This is why nitrates are used with great caution in inferior MI."}, {"q": "Which findings indicate LEFT-sided heart failure? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Crackles on auscultation", "Jugular venous distension", "Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea", "Peripheral oedema and ascites", "Orthopnoea"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "<b>Left goes to the Lungs</b>: crackles, orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, frothy pink sputum. <b>Right goes to the Rest of the body</b>: JVD, peripheral oedema, ascites, hepatomegaly. The commonest cause of right-sided failure is left-sided failure, so many clients eventually show both."}, {"q": "A client with heart failure is taught to weigh daily. Which finding should be reported?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A gain of 2 to 3 pounds in 24 hours", "A loss of 1 pound in a week", "A stable weight for five days", "A gain of 1 pound over two weeks"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Daily weight is the earliest and most reliable sign of fluid retention</b> - it changes before the swelling and the breathlessness do. A gain of 2 to 3 lb in a day, or 5 lb in a week, is the reportable threshold. One pint of retained fluid is about one pound."}, {"q": "A client has hypotension, muffled heart sounds and distended neck veins after chest trauma. What is occurring?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Cardiogenic shock from infarction", "Pulmonary embolism"], "ans": [1], "why": "Those three findings are <b>Beck triad</b>, and they mean cardiac tamponade: fluid in the pericardial sac is squeezing the heart so it cannot fill. Tension pneumothorax also gives hypotension and JVD, but with <b>absent breath sounds</b> and <b>tracheal deviation</b> rather than muffled heart sounds."}, {"q": "Aortic stenosis produces a murmur during which phase, and what is the classic symptom triad?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diastole; fatigue, palpitations, oedema", "Systole; chest pain, syncope, dyspnoea", "Diastole; cough, haemoptysis, orthopnoea", "Systole; fever, rash, arthralgia"], "ans": [1], "why": "The aortic valve <i>should</i> be open during <b>systole</b>, so a stenotic one obstructs then and murmurs then. The classic triad is <b>chest pain, syncope and dyspnoea</b>, and it carries a risk of sudden death. Work out when the valve should be open or shut and the murmur timing follows."}], "11": [{"q": "A client admitted with severe dehydration has a BUN of 62 mg/dL and a creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL. What does this ratio suggest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Intrarenal injury from a nephrotoxic drug", "Prerenal azotaemia", "Postrenal obstruction", "Chronic kidney disease stage 4"], "ans": [1], "why": "A BUN:creatinine ratio above about <b>20:1</b> points at a <b>prerenal</b> cause - the kidney tissue is intact, it is just not being perfused. Here the ratio is over 40:1. BUN rises with dehydration, GI bleeding, high protein intake and steroids, so it is far less specific than creatinine."}, {"q": "Which are the most common causes of chronic kidney disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Glomerulonephritis and polycystic kidney disease", "Diabetes and hypertension", "Kidney stones and recurrent UTI", "NSAID use and dehydration"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Diabetes and hypertension</b> together account for most cases. Both damage the small vessels the glomerulus depends on, and both are themselves worsened by the failing kidney - the kidney's own renin drives the blood pressure higher, which damages it further."}, {"q": "A client in the oliguric phase of acute kidney injury has a potassium of 6.9 mEq/L with peaked T waves. Why is this the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It causes severe muscle cramping", "It will progress to a lethal cardiac dysrhythmia", "It indicates the kidneys are recovering", "It causes metabolic alkalosis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hyperkalaemia is what kills in the oliguric phase, and it does it through the <b>heart</b> - peaked T waves progress to a widening QRS and then a sine wave pattern and arrest. It needs treating immediately, not at the next round. The kidney cannot excrete potassium, so it accumulates fast."}, {"q": "Which findings distinguish nephrotic syndrome from nephritic syndrome? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Nephrotic has massive proteinuria over 3.5 g per day", "Nephritic characteristically shows cola-coloured haematuria", "Nephrotic typically causes hypertension", "Nephrotic causes severe oedema from low albumin", "Nephritic often follows a streptococcal infection"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Nephr<b>O</b>tic is pr<b>O</b>tein and <b>O</b>edema, with a normal or low blood pressure. Nephr<b>I</b>tic is <b>I</b>nflammation and blood, and it is the one that causes <b>hypertension</b>. Nephritic often follows a strep infection by one to three weeks."}, {"q": "Which hormone deficiency explains the anaemia of chronic kidney disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Aldosterone", "Erythropoietin", "Antidiuretic hormone", "Parathyroid hormone"], "ans": [1], "why": "The kidney makes <b>erythropoietin</b>, which tells the marrow to produce red cells. Without it the client develops a <b>normocytic</b> anaemia that iron alone will not correct - it is treated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents. The kidney also makes renin and activates vitamin D, which is why CKD causes hypertension and bone disease too."}, {"q": "A client reports sudden severe flank pain radiating to the groin, with haematuria, and cannot lie still. What is the most likely cause?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pyelonephritis", "Renal calculi", "Bladder infection", "Glomerulonephritis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Colicky <b>flank pain radiating to the groin</b> with haematuria, in a client who cannot get comfortable, is a stone. Restlessness is the clue - clients with <b>peritoneal</b> irritation lie completely still, while colic makes people writhe. Pyelonephritis gives fever, chills and CVA tenderness instead."}], "12": [{"q": "A client with a large pulmonary embolism remains hypoxic despite high-flow oxygen. Which concept explains this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dead space: ventilation without perfusion", "Shunt: perfusion without ventilation", "Diffusion defect across a thickened membrane", "Hypoventilation from sedation"], "ans": [0], "why": "A PE blocks <b>perfusion</b> while the alveoli are still being ventilated - that is <b>dead space</b>. Oxygen reaches alveoli that have no blood flow past them to pick it up. A <b>shunt</b> is the reverse: blood flows past alveoli that are not ventilated, as in pneumonia or atelectasis, and it is equally resistant to supplemental oxygen."}, {"q": "During a severe asthma attack, the client's wheezing stops and the chest becomes silent. How should the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The bronchodilator has worked", "The client is improving and can be stepped down", "Too little air is moving to generate sound; respiratory arrest is near", "The client is holding their breath"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>silent chest is an emergency</b>, not an improvement. Wheeze requires airflow through a narrowed airway; when the obstruction becomes severe enough there is not enough air moving to make any sound at all. Improvement looks like <i>louder</i> air entry with less wheeze, not silence."}, {"q": "Which findings suggest a tension pneumothorax? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Absent breath sounds on the affected side", "Tracheal deviation toward the affected side", "Tracheal deviation away from the affected side", "Jugular venous distension", "Hypotension"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Pressure builds on the affected side and pushes the mediastinum <b>away</b> from it, compressing the opposite lung and the great vessels - hence JVD and hypotension. Deviation <i>toward</i> the affected side happens with atelectasis or lung collapse from lost volume, which pulls rather than pushes."}, {"q": "What distinguishes obstructive from restrictive lung disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Obstructive limits getting air in; restrictive limits getting air out", "Obstructive limits getting air out; restrictive limits lung expansion", "Both reduce the FEV1/FVC ratio equally", "Restrictive disease always involves the airways"], "ans": [1], "why": "Obstructive disease traps air - the problem is getting it <b>out</b>, so the FEV1/FVC ratio falls and residual volume rises. Restrictive disease prevents the lung <b>expanding</b>, so all volumes fall together while the ratio stays normal or even rises. Asthma and COPD obstruct; fibrosis and neuromuscular disease restrict."}, {"q": "Which type of glaucoma presents as sudden severe eye pain with halos, a fixed mid-dilated pupil and vomiting?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Open-angle glaucoma", "Closed-angle glaucoma", "Cataract", "Macular degeneration"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Closed-angle</b> glaucoma is sudden, painful and an emergency - the pressure must come down or the vision is lost. <b>Open-angle</b> is gradual and painless and takes <b>peripheral</b> vision first, which is why it is often found late. Macular degeneration takes <b>central</b> vision instead - the opposite pattern."}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. Which precautions are required?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Contact precautions", "Droplet precautions", "Airborne precautions with a negative-pressure room", "Standard precautions only"], "ans": [2], "why": "TB spreads on <b>airborne</b> droplet nuclei that stay suspended, so it needs a negative-pressure room and a fitted respirator - not a surgical mask. Droplet precautions would be appropriate for influenza or pertussis, which travel on larger droplets that fall quickly."}], "13": [{"q": "A client reports epigastric pain that is RELIEVED by eating and returns two to three hours later, often waking them at night. Which is most likely?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Gastric ulcer", "Duodenal ulcer", "Cholecystitis", "Pancreatitis"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Duodenal</b> ulcer pain is <b>relieved</b> by food and returns once the stomach empties, classically waking the client at night. <b>Gastric</b> ulcer pain is <b>worse</b> with eating, which is why those clients lose weight. That single relationship separates the two more reliably than the location of the pain."}, {"q": "Which features are characteristic of Crohn disease rather than ulcerative colitis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Can affect anywhere from mouth to anus", "Skip lesions with normal bowel in between", "Continuous inflammation starting at the rectum", "Full-thickness inflammation causing fistulas", "Bloody diarrhoea is the hallmark symptom"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Crohn is <b>patchy, deep and anywhere</b> - skip lesions, full thickness, fistulas, strictures and B12 malabsorption from terminal ileum involvement. Ulcerative colitis is <b>continuous, shallow and colon only</b>, and bloody diarrhoea is its hallmark. Depth explains the complications of each."}, {"q": "A client with abdominal pain is lying completely still and resists any movement. The abdomen is rigid with rebound tenderness and absent bowel sounds. What does this suggest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Bowel obstruction", "Peritonitis", "Renal colic", "Gastroenteritis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Peritoneal irritation makes any movement agonising, so the client <b>lies completely still</b> - with a rigid abdomen, rebound tenderness and absent bowel sounds. A client with <b>colic</b>, from obstruction or a stone, cannot get comfortable and writhes. How the client is lying is diagnostic before you touch them."}, {"q": "Which precautions are required for a client with Clostridioides difficile diarrhoea?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Standard precautions and alcohol hand gel", "Contact precautions and handwashing with soap and water", "Airborne precautions", "Droplet precautions and alcohol hand gel"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Contact precautions</b>, and hands must be washed with <b>soap and water</b> - alcohol gel does <b>not</b> kill C. difficile spores. It typically follows antibiotic use, which wipes out the normal flora that would otherwise keep it in check."}, {"q": "A client has periumbilical pain that migrates to the right lower quadrant, with anorexia and rebound tenderness. Which condition is most likely?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diverticulitis", "Appendicitis", "Cholecystitis", "Pancreatitis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pain that starts periumbilically and <b>migrates to the right lower quadrant</b> at McBurney point, with anorexia and rebound tenderness, is appendicitis. <b>Diverticulitis</b> is the mirror image on the <b>left</b>. Cholecystitis is right upper quadrant after a fatty meal; pancreatitis is epigastric and radiates to the back."}, {"q": "Which is the most common cause of a small bowel obstruction?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Colorectal tumour", "Adhesions from previous surgery", "Volvulus", "Diverticular disease"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Adhesions</b> from previous abdominal surgery are the commonest cause in the small bowel, followed by hernia. <b>Tumour</b> is the leading cause in the <b>large</b> bowel. Small bowel obstruction vomits early and profusely; large bowel distends markedly and vomits late."}], "14": [{"q": "A client with cirrhosis is increasingly confused, with a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands. Which mechanism explains this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Low albumin causing cerebral oedema", "Ammonia accumulating because the liver cannot convert it to urea", "Bilirubin deposition in the brain", "Hypoglycaemia from glycogen depletion"], "ans": [1], "why": "The failing liver cannot convert <b>ammonia</b> to urea, so it reaches the brain: confusion, sleep reversal, <b>asterixis</b> - the flapping tremor - and eventually coma. Lactulose treats it by trapping ammonia in the gut, aiming for two to three soft stools a day, and the measure of success is that the client's thinking clears."}, {"q": "Which consequences of portal hypertension would the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Oesophageal varices", "Ascites", "Splenomegaly with thrombocytopenia", "Polycythaemia", "Caput medusae"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Blood cannot get through the scarred liver, so it reroutes through small veins that were never built for it - varices in the oesophagus and around the umbilicus. Pressure also enlarges the <b>spleen</b>, which then destroys platelets. <b>Polycythaemia</b> is the opposite of what happens; these clients are anaemic and thrombocytopenic."}, {"q": "Which hepatitis viruses are transmitted primarily by the faecal-oral route?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A and E", "B and C", "C and D", "B and D"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Vowels from the bowels</b> - hepatitis <b>A and E</b> come from contaminated food and water and do not become chronic. B, C and D are <b>blood-borne</b> (B and D also sexually and perinatally), and B, C and D can all become chronic. There is a vaccine for A and B but none for C."}, {"q": "A client has severe epigastric pain radiating straight through to the back, relieved by leaning forward, with vomiting. Which laboratory result best supports the likely diagnosis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevated lipase", "Elevated troponin", "Elevated ammonia", "Elevated alkaline phosphatase alone"], "ans": [0], "why": "Epigastric pain boring <b>through to the back</b>, eased by leaning forward, is pancreatitis, and <b>lipase</b> is the more specific marker (amylase also rises but is less specific). The two dominant causes are <b>gallstones and alcohol</b>. Cullen and Grey Turner signs indicate a haemorrhagic course."}, {"q": "A 24-year-old reports sudden severe testicular pain with swelling and a high-riding testis. What is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply ice and reassess in the morning", "Immediate surgical evaluation for torsion", "Prescribe antibiotics for epididymitis", "Schedule an outpatient ultrasound next week"], "ans": [1], "why": "Testicular torsion is a <b>surgical emergency with a window of roughly six hours</b> before the testis is lost. Sudden agonising pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex means operate now. Contrast this with a <b>painless</b> hard lump in a young man, which is investigated for cancer but is not an emergency."}, {"q": "A postmenopausal client reports light vaginal bleeding. What is the appropriate interpretation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Normal for the first few years after menopause", "Expected if she is taking calcium supplements", "Abnormal and requires investigation for endometrial cancer", "Normal if it occurs only once"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Any</b> postmenopausal bleeding is abnormal and must be investigated, however light and however brief. Menopause is defined as 12 consecutive months without a period, so bleeding after that point is by definition unexpected. Endometrial cancer is the diagnosis being excluded."}]};
